{
  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-75933",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "We note that $m=0$ cannot be a solution since division by $0$ is undefined. \n\nThe equation has the denominators $2m$ and $3m.$ So first, we find the least common multiple (or $\\text{LCM}$) of the denominators. We find that\n\n$$\n\\text{LCM}(2m,3m)=6m.\n$$\n\nSo, we multiply both sides of the equation by $6m,$ and simplify: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3m+2}{2m}-\\frac{m+2}{3m} & =1 \\\\ 6m\\cdot (\\frac{3m+2}{2m}-\\frac{m+2}{3m}) & =6m\\cdot (1) \\\\ 6m\\cdot \\frac{3m+2}{2m}-6m\\cdot \\frac{m+2}{3m} & =6m\\cdot 1 \\\\ 3\\cdot \\cancel{2m}\\cdot \\frac{3m+2}{\\cancel{2m}}-2\\cdot \\cancel{3m}\\cdot \\frac{m+2}{\\cancel{3m}} & =6m \\\\ 3(3m+2)-2(m+2) & =6m\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now have a linear equation in $m,$ and we can solve this using the addition and multiplication principles:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3(3m+2)-2(m+2) & =6m \\\\ 9m+6-2m-4 & =6m \\\\ 7m+2 & =6m \\\\ m & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we recall that we cannot allow $m=0$ as a solution. But since $m=-2\\ne 0,$ we conclude that the solution is $m=-2.$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
  "fields": [],
  "problem_field_keys": [],
  "answer_fields_complete": null,
